integrated_rules:
Reusable decision rule (outcome mapping):

1) NOT GOOD FOR SOCIETY if there is substantial evidence that AI use/deployment (e.g., via opacity/unverifiability and profiling/automation) substantially undermines one or more of the following values in ways that track AG2’s mechanisms and targets:
- critical thinking and autonomy/independence because users cannot reliably verify or challenge AI outputs;
- non-discrimination/equal autonomy for racial minorities because opacity makes racial bias hard to detect and correct, allowing discriminatory outcomes to persist or worsen;
- privacy because opacity and profiling capabilities make privacy harms hard for individuals to control, enabling persistent privacy exposure;
- broad economic security/economic well-being because automation/displacement and advantage concentration harm many people and businesses.

2) GOOD FOR SOCIETY if AI use demonstrates the kinds of benefits cited by AG1 (improves everyday life by making tasks more enjoyable and convenient; improves health and raises the standard of living; makes work easier for students and professionals, improving learning and productivity; improves accessibility for marginalized groups—especially people with disabilities; improves workplace safety) AND it is not the case (in the overall assessment) that these demonstrated benefits are outweighed by overriding societal harms.

3) If the evidence does not clearly satisfy (1) or (2), leave the assessment unresolved (no conclusion).
